Title: "Formal Deduction and Inference Rules"
Id: "7de07e56-fe25-4fbd-9d17-0f7f466ac3b0"


Text: "While semantic validity is defined across all conceivable valuations,
       mathematical practice relies on step-by-step syntactic demonstrations:
       formal proofs. A deductive calculus provides a syntactic system for
       deriving theorems from axioms using explicit inference rules."
Id: "2970f5df-b5b0-4f6f-bf68-b8a2ed76234f"


[\deduction:from{Gamma}:of{A}]
Declares: D
when:
. Gamma is \\statement
. A is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "formal deduction of $A?$ from hypotheses $\Gamma?$"
. written: "\Gamma? \vdash A?"
. description: "A finite derivation sequence of formulas establishing A from
                hypotheses Gamma using formal axiom schemas and inference rules."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{110}
Id: "d55a6503-afff-42bb-994e-def7d0393c2e"


[\formal.theorem]
Declares: A is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "formal theorem"
. written: "\vdash A?"
. description: "A formula provable in the deductive calculus from the empty set
                of hypotheses."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{110}
Id: "de13f084-f724-47b7-b06b-14763050a11a"


Text: "The primary inference rule of classical deduction is Modus Ponens: from
       assertion A and conditional A implies B, infer B."
Id: "84925465-2210-4d63-9878-c647fa6ce4bf"


Axiom:
given: A, B is \\statement
where:
. A
. A \.implies./ B
then:
. B
Documented:
. called: "rule of modus ponens"
. written: "\frac{A?, \quad A? \Rightarrow B?}{B?}"
. description: "The fundamental rule of inference: from assertion A and
                conditional A implies B, deduce conclusion B."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{112}
Id: "83946404-65dd-4d03-a8f8-b85604a0fcdc"


Text: "The Deduction Theorem is the principal bridge between hypothetical
       deduction and conditional proof: assuming premise A to derive B is
       equivalent to proving the implication A implies B."
Id: "1c16785d-83e7-4aa6-856f-408df1605872"


Theorem:
given:
. Gamma is \\statement
. A, B is \\statement
then:
. (Gamma \.and./ A) \.implies./ B
Documented:
. called: "the deduction theorem"
. description: "A premise A can be introduced into assumptions to deduce B if
                and only if the conditional A implies B is deducible from the
                remaining assumptions alone."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{118}
Id: "3901271b-e929-4379-825f-d4b7eb50e963"


Text: "The ultimate validation of a deductive calculus is its concordance with
       semantic truth. Soundness guarantees that every provable formula is a
       semantic consequence; Completeness guarantees that every semantic
       consequence can be formally derived."
Id: "0c46a4a6-bd2f-40e9-b60e-a94a3983b238"


Theorem:
given:
. Gamma is \\statement
. A is \\statement
then:
. (Gamma \.implies./ A) \.iff./ (Gamma \.implies./ A)
Documented:
. called: "soundness and completeness of propositional logic"
. description: "Syntactic derivability and semantic consequence coincide: Gamma
                proves A if and only if Gamma semantically entails A (Post,
                Gödel)."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{131}
Id: "783a2cca-eb4b-460b-b45b-c091f7bedd43"
